      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4\\cdot 0-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercept occurs at the point $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275039/assets/87766af5840b6b3729fa200d383e18c5f7926d9000633f99b481e8b4a6922732.png)",
